"in a TemplateVM / AppVM combination, this registry fix has to be applied to "
"the TemplateVM, as the ``HKLM`` registry key belongs to the template-based "
"part of the registry."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/templates/windows/qubes-windows-tools.rst:334
#: 3ed6806c41c94676a0c0edd87fa1b043
msgid ""
"**Windows 10 and 11:**  If the experimental graphics driver is installed and "
"enabled by setting the ``qvm-features`` parameter ``gui`` to 1, seamless "
"mode can be enabled and will stay enabled even after reboot of the qube."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/templates/windows/qubes-windows-tools.rst:338
#: 729dec360e994d9ca5857002a2dec3fe
msgid "Xen PV drivers and Qubes Windows Tools"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/templates/windows/qubes-windows-tools.rst:341
#: a2a91e6b36674559bdb87ee0bd73a0e9
msgid ""
"Installing Xen’s PV drivers in the VM will lower its resources usage when "
"using network and/or I/O intensive applications, but *may* come at the price "
"of system stability (although Xen’s PV drivers on a Windows VM are usually "
"very stable). They can be installed as an optional part of Qubes Windows "
"Tools (QWT), which bundles Xen’s PV drivers."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/templates/windows/qubes-windows-tools.rst:343
#: 3f6a5c5d56e749c68f940b9ca8f229fb
msgid "**Notes** about using Xen’s VBD (storage) PV driver:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/templates/windows/qubes-windows-tools.rst:345
#: b0d94535b45644f0871649c79cf74775
msgid ""
"**Windows 7:** Installing the driver requires a fully updated VM, or else "
"you’ll likely get a BSOD (“Blue Screen Of Death”) and a VM in a difficult-to-"
"fix state. Updating Windows takes *hours* and for casual usage there isn’t "
"much of a performance between the disk PV driver and the default one; so "
"there is likely no need to go through the lengthy Windows Update process if "
"your VM doesn’t have access to untrusted networks and if you don’t use I/O "
"intensive apps or attach block devices. If you plan to update your newly "
"installed Windows VM, it is recommended that you do so *before* installing "
"Qubes Windows Tools. Installing the driver will probably cause Windows 7 "
"activation to become invalid, but the activation can be restored using the "
"Microsoft telephone activation method."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/templates/windows/qubes-windows-tools.rst:347
#: a0a8c7e1ba024f7aa77467a7ed43f8de
msgid ""
"The option to install the storage PV driver is disabled by default in Qubes "
"Windows Tools"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/templates/windows/qubes-windows-tools.rst:349
#: 3244d8c063374f66b1ea13197b38eb4c
msgid ""
"In case you already had QWT installed without the storage PV driver and you "
"then updated the VM, you may then install the driver by again starting the "
"QWT installer and selecting the change option."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/templates/windows/qubes-windows-tools.rst:354
#: 2ccc796100684881a134a601c73a50e6
msgid "Using Windows AppVMs in seamless mode"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/templates/windows/qubes-windows-tools.rst:357
#: 7ec6f94c166441da8d93959d0940670d
msgid ""
"Windows Apps can be started using the Qubes menu. Alternatively, you can "
"open the Windows menu by typing the Windows key on your keyboard while the "
"cursor is positioned in a window of the Windows VM."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/templates/windows/qubes-windows-tools.rst:359
#: edecdd35a8fe48289532f8293f5782d4
msgid ""
"**Note:** The following features are only available for Windows 7 and are "
"still somewhat buggy in Windows 10 and 11."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/templates/windows/qubes-windows-tools.rst:361
#: cf1042e6035148f096068cbd3366192c
msgid ""
"Once you start a Windows-based AppVM with Qubes Tools installed, you can "
"easily start individual applications from the VM (note the ``-a`` switch "
"used here, which will auto-start the VM if it is not running):"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/templates/windows/qubes-windows-tools.rst:369
#: e0ba09b30fb549aab64e19f88512c8f9
msgid "|windows-seamless-4.png| |windows-seamless-1.png|"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/templates/windows/qubes-windows-tools.rst:693
#: 869382fb3507411ea40157c3dc5453cc
msgid "windows-seamless-4.png"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/templates/windows/qubes-windows-tools.rst:696
#: f105deb5aa1a4c788dc41fe393f492e6
msgid "windows-seamless-1.png"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/templates/windows/qubes-windows-tools.rst:371
#: ec5aa018b8684c0186aceefe5c4dcb95
msgid ""
"Also, the inter-VM services work as usual – e.g. to request opening a "
"document or URL in the Windows AppVM from another VM:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/templates/windows/qubes-windows-tools.rst:380
#: f93949eebc224f3aba6c4e044a1c7a40
msgid ""
"… just like in the case of Linux AppVMs. Of course all those operations are "
"governed by central policy engine running in Dom0 – if the policy doesn’t "
"contain explicit rules for the source and/or target AppVM, the user will be "
"asked whether to allow or deny the operation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/templates/windows/qubes-windows-tools.rst:382
#: fb56b73c776041bb9053540a94164058
msgid ""
"Inter-VM file copy and clipboard works for Windows AppVMs the same way as "
"for Linux AppVM (except that we don’t provide a command line wrapper, ``qvm-"
"copy-to-vm`` in Windows VMs) – to copy files from Windows AppVMs just right-"
"click on the file in Explorer, and choose: Send To-> Other AppVM."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/templates/windows/qubes-windows-tools.rst:384
#: 32050aa7b283440ea2470520040a4fd6
msgid ""
"To simulate :kbd:`Ctrl-Alt-Delete` in the HVM (SAS, Secure Attention "
"Sequence), press Ctrl-Alt-Home while having any window of this VM in the "
"foreground."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/templates/windows/qubes-windows-tools.rst:386
#: 91348a6539b24082abec1c45e256a02e
msgid "|windows-seamless-7.png|"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/templates/windows/qubes-windows-tools.rst:699
